Pass A verify-delivery failed one check: "Reserve crash: the unacknowledged OrderPlaced is back in its queue" (OrderPlaced=0 after 20 s). The crash is a Process.Kill() before the acknowledgement code runs, and the replay that follows proves the message was not lost.

--- A/B: verify-delivery, 10 valid runs each, fixed and unmodified code alternated, concurrency 1 ---
Both copies of verify-delivery.ps1 carried the same scratch-only patch (not committed): the requeue check records the seconds until OrderPlaced is back and a 1 s trace of ready/unacked/consumers, and keeps the same 20 s pass limit.
fixed: 10 valid runs, crash check failures 0, other failures 0, requeue seconds 1,3.1,1,0,0,1,4.1,3.1,4.1,6.1
unmodified: 10 valid runs, crash check failures 0, other failures 1, requeue seconds 3.1,0,1,0,0,2,2.1,4.1,4.1,3.1
The one failure on unmodified code is the same kind of race in another consumer: "OrderProcessed crash: the unacknowledged OrderProcessed is back in its queue" (OrderProcessed=0). So the requeue-after-crash checks are timing-sensitive on unmodified code too; the fixed code does not fail them more often.
